@meta
id: 28-12
title: Drill — write the mapping, comment above
chapter: 28
type: drill
skills: vimrc-mappings
par: 110
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: nothing — keybindings.json is JSON and you would paste it
open: .vimrc

@brief
Eight times: add the buffer-list mapping to the end of the Mappings
section, with its reason on the line above, and save.

    " <leader>b lists the buffers and waits for a number
    nnoremap <leader>b :ls<CR>:b<Space>

The order you type them in is the drill. Mapping first, comment second,
because `O` from a comment line gives you another comment leader you did
not ask for.

@keys
| `G` `o` | open a line at the bottom |
| `O` | open a line above |
| `<CR>` `<Space>` | how a mapping spells Enter and Space |

@hints
`Go`, the mapping, Esc, then `O` and the comment.
--
`<leader>`, `<CR>` and `<Space>` are typed literally — vim reads the
angle brackets, you do not press the keys.

@solution
    G o nnoremap <leader>b :ls<CR>:b<Space> <Esc>
    O " <leader>b lists the buffers and waits for a number <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The mapping is genuinely useful: it lists the buffers and leaves `:b `
sitting on the command line, so you read the number off the list and type
it. Two keys to switch to any file you have open.

It also shows the shape of most good mappings: they do not do the job,
they set the job up and leave you in the right place. A mapping that
runs `:ls` and then guesses which buffer you meant would be wrong half
the time and you would stop trusting it.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Mappings ---
let mapleader = " "
nnoremap Y y$
nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Mappings ---
let mapleader = " "
nnoremap Y y$
nnoremap <leader>w :w<CR>
" <leader>b lists the buffers and waits for a number
nnoremap <leader>b :ls<CR>:b<Space>
